import VersoBlueprint
import Comparator.Solution

open Verso.Genre Verso.Genre.Manual Informal

#doc (Manual) "Checking the independent statement" =>

%%%
tag := "verification"
%%%

The challenge defines the two permutations of the complex plane directly from their disk
actions. It asks one question using only these definitions, the radical
$`\sqrt{3+(1+\sqrt5)/2}`, and Mathlib. The solution identifies that group with the formal
development and proves the statement. Open the Lean panel to compare the precise statement with
the {bpref "critical_radius_five"}[fivefold critical radius] of the geometric argument.

:::theorem "challenge_finite" (lean := "FiveDisk.finite_iff")
For every real $`r`, the group generated by the clockwise fifth-turns of the closed disks of
radius $`r` about $`-1` and $`1` is finite if and only if $`r<\sqrt{3+(1+\sqrt5)/2}`.
:::

:::proof "challenge_finite"
Both groups are generated by the same two permutations, so the challenge's group at radius
$`r` is $`GG_5(r)`. The statement is then the equivalence in the
{bpref "critical_radius_five"}[fivefold critical radius].
:::

This is the sharp form of the result: the group is finite below the threshold and infinite at
the threshold and above it. The negative check `scripts/check-comparator-negative.sh` makes two
mutations of the challenge, and Comparator must reject each: replacing $`<` by $`\le`, and
changing the definition of the radius.

*Independent replay*

Each successful hosted build provides a verification record identifying the commit, Actions run,
toolchain, kernel checks and theorem names. The [site home](/index.html) and the
[Actions history](https://github.com/eric-vergo/TDCSG/actions) are available independently.
A local preview can be viewed without a hosted verdict. The Project Stats page describes what the blueprint
inspected in its compiled declarations. The release workflow separately runs the comparator
on Linux and publishes its verdict with the site.

Read the [standalone challenge](https://github.com/eric-vergo/TDCSG/blob/main/Comparator/Challenge.lean),
[solution](https://github.com/eric-vergo/TDCSG/blob/main/Comparator/Solution.lean), and
[allowed axioms](https://github.com/eric-vergo/TDCSG/blob/main/comparator.json). The challenge
compares formal statements. Checking how those statements express the intended mathematical
problem also requires reading their definitions.

